<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cannon">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cannon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cannon is a large-calibre gun, a type of artillery firing a projectile with an explosive propellant, first securely attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the 13th century. It probably developed from or alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">fire lance</a>: Yuan troops are recorded using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-cannon">hand cannons</a> in 1288, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Xanadu</a> Gun of 1298 is the earliest extant cannon with a date of production. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> was its main propellant until the late 19th century; by the end of the 14th century cannons were widespread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, and in 1453 Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> broke the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cannon.jpg" alt="Cannon" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Saint-Lys)_Fauconneau_de_1589.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its army included infantry, war elephants and a fleet of large oared galleys, and from the 1560s it obtained <b>cannon</b>, gunners and military experts from the Ottoman Empire, which Sultan Alauddin al-Kahhar had approached for help against the Portuguese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The change came when Ahmad adopted matchlock firearms and, later, <b>cannon</b> obtained through Arabia and the Ottomans, and organised his army to use them well.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Armies fought mainly as infantry armed with bows, swords, spears and, from the sixteenth century, matchlocks and <b>cannon</b>, and they made skilful use of earthen ramparts, stockades and the dense forests and rivers of the valley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam of the Shafi'i school became central to court and urban life, while Brunei's brass-casters became famous across the region for their <b>cannon</b> and vessels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungars cast <b>cannon</b> with the help of Johan Gustaf Renat, a Swedish officer captured by Russia at Poltava in 1709 who later fell into Dzungar hands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Goryeo supplied ships and troops for the failed Yuan invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281, and late in the dynasty Choe Mu-seon's gunpowder office armed ships with <b>cannon</b> that destroyed a Japanese pirate fleet at Jinpo in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 1288</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Estimates for the first true cannon run from about 1200 to 1280; the Xanadu Gun of 1298 is the oldest with a date inscribed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze<span>c. 4650 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">Cast iron<span>8th century BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">Fire lance<span>950</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">Arquebus<span>1411</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/turtle-ship">Turtle ship<span>1413</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cannon">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
